Stefan Walldorf wrote:
> Does anyone know, how exactly the chances if a thrown/fired missile
> disappears, is calculated? As far as I know, it depends on 3 variables:
> 
> BUC-status of the missile
> Luck
> Enchantment of the missile
> 
> Now I would like to calculate the exact chance of, say, a +7 blessed
> missile disappear, if my luck is at 13, or whatever other imaginable
> combination.

I am surprised there isn't a spoiler on this, so I have done a little 
investigation.  The code is on lines 1317 to 1341 of dothrow.c, in the 
middle of thitmonst().

1317. 	/* projectiles other than magic stones
1318. 	   sometimes disappear when thrown */
1319. 	if (objects[otyp].oc_skill < P_NONE &&
1320. 			objects[otyp].oc_skill > -P_BOOMERANG &&
1321. 			!objects[otyp].oc_magic) {
1322. 	    /* we were breaking 2/3 of everything unconditionally.
1323. 	     * we still don't want anything to survive unconditionally,
1324. 	     * but we need ammo to stay around longer on average.
1325. 	     */
1326. 	    int broken, chance;
1327. 	    chance = 3 + greatest_erosion(obj) - obj->spe;
1328. 	    if (chance > 1)
1329. 		broken = rn2(chance);
1330. 	    else
1331. 		broken = !rn2(4);
1332. 	    if (obj->blessed && !rnl(4))
1333. 		broken = 0;
1334.
1335. 	    if (broken) {
1336. 		if (*u.ushops)
1337. 		    check_shop_obj(obj, bhitpos.x,bhitpos.y, TRUE);
1338. 		obfree(obj, (struct obj *)0);
1339. 		return 1;
1340. 	    }
1341. 	}

If your skill is sufficiently low (barring some special case to do with 
boomerangs) and the missile is not "magical", then:
there is a 1-in-(greatest rusting/burntness/rottenness level minus 
enchantment plus 3) chance that it won't break, unless that number is 
under 2, in which case there is a 3/4 chance that it won't break.  In 
any case, if it's blessed, there is a 1-in-4 chance that the decision 
will get overridden and it won't break anyway.

Yuk.  So the overall effect is complicated.  With a little probability work:

If the enchantment of the object minus its maximum erosion is at least 
+2, then the chance of breaking is 1/4 for non-blessed objects and 3/16 
for blessed ones.

OTOH, if that adjusted enchantment is less than +2, then:
  *  if non-blessed: there is a 1-in-(3 minus it) chance that it *won't* 
break
  *  if blessed: the chance of survival is 3/4 of the one for 
non-blessed case + 1/4 (i.e. boosted a bit and at least 1/4).

Unless I've got it wrong.

